From dc0547992018a219f386e10227ccde01e4c4e9ec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 09:53:56 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 202/209] fix(area): distributor - end-cap-only placement, node
 registration, rebuild storm

A five-point spline produced exactly two instances, one at each end of the curve
and nothing in between. Three things came out of chasing that.

1. The shape of the symptom IS the end-cap path. The Meshes list is what gets
distributed ALONG the curve; End Cap Mesh places exactly one instance at each
end of an open curve and nothing between them. A distributor whose only mesh
sits in End Cap Mesh therefore produces precisely two objects at the two ends -
and said nothing about it, while the end cap is a single inviting mesh picker
and the list needs a row to be added first. WarnIfNothingToDistribute() now says
so once, naming the mistake, and separately reports "none of the N meshes could
be loaded" and "the list is empty".

The placement arithmetic itself was re-derived outside the engine against the
same formulas (five points spanning ~48 m -> segments 11.5/14.8/16.2/14.7, total
57.3 m, Fixed Step 4 m -> 15 instances), so TotalLength, the arc-length table and
the step count are not the problem.

2. Render nodes were registered on every instance of every rebuild.
CBrush::SetMatrix already unregisters and re-registers a node whenever its
matrix really changes (Brush.cpp:224-227), and that is also the only place
CalcBBox() runs, so the world box the octree indexes by comes from there. The
extra RegisterEntity() per apply re-indexed nodes the engine had just indexed.
It is now done exactly once per node, on its first apply, which is what
CEntitySlot::UpdateRenderNode does (EntitySlot.cpp:213-220) - and which also
covers the matching trap on the other side: SetMatrix EARLY-OUTS on an unchanged
matrix (Brush.cpp:203-204), so a brand new node whose instance matrix is the
identity it was constructed with would otherwise never register at all.

3. Rebuilds were a storm. ENTITY_EVENT_COMPONENT_PROPERTY_CHANGED arrives on
every inspector serialization pass, not only when a value changed, so an open
property panel tore every render node down and built it again many times a
second - reloading stat objects and re-registering nodes each time.
ComputeBuildSignature() digests every parameter, every mesh path and the curve's
length and closed flag, and a rebuild whose signature is unchanged now returns
immediately. OnShapeChanged forces one, because a point can move without
changing the curve's length.

Diagnostics, as asked: every real rebuild logs one line - curve length, spacing
mode with its step/count/density, trim, skip, alignment, meshes listed vs
loaded, whether an end cap is loaded, how many instances were asked for, how
many were placed and how many nodes exist. "Nothing appeared" is now readable
from the console without a debugger.

